mited to football hooliganism. But I maintain that the proper target of the criticism is that specific cultural strand, not the British people as a whole. The leap from "stag parties in Prague" to "British people generally" is the same logical error I've been pointing to all along, just applied to a wider piece of evidence. The reputation has a real source, but that source is a recurring behaviour pattern by a specific demographic in specific leisure settings, not a national personality trait.
